DevOps Internship - Week 4 Report

Topic : Docker & Docker Compose

-> Introduction
Week 4 focuses on Docker and Docker Compose. The internship document covers Docker architecture,
installation, images, containers, Docker Hub, Dockerfile, volumes, networks, environment variables, port
mapping, multi-container applications and Docker best practices. The main learning approach is to
understand how containers work and practice managing them.


-> What is Docker?
Docker is a containerization platform used to package applications and their dependencies into portable
and consistent containers. Containerization makes applications easier to run across different
environments and is an important part of modern DevOps.

-> Docker Architecture
Docker uses a client and daemon model. The Docker client sends commands, while the Docker daemon
manages images, containers, networks and volumes. Docker registries are used to store and distribute
images.

-> Docker Images vs Containers
A Docker image is a package or template used to create containers. A Docker container is a running or
stopped instance created from an image. Images are built or downloaded, while containers are started,
stopped and managed.

-> Dockerfile
A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions for building a Docker image. The hands-on activity
requires creating a Dockerfile for a simple HTML or Node.js application and building a custom image.

-> Docker Volumes
Docker volumes provide persistent storage. They are useful when application data should remain
available even if a container is recreated. The hands-on activity requires persistent storage using Docker
volumes.

-> Docker Networks
Docker networks allow containers to communicate. A custom Docker network is required for the
multi-container application so that frontend, backend and database services can communicate
successfully.

-> Docker Compose
Docker Compose is used to define and run multiple containers as one application using a Compose file.
The Week 4 activity requires a frontend, backend and database together with volumes and a custom
network.

-> Docker Hub
Docker Hub is a registry where Docker images can be stored and shared. The assignment requires
pulling images from Docker Hub and pushing the custom Docker image created during the activity.

-> Testing and Verification
Docker installation, images and running containers should be verified. The Dockerfile should build
successfully, the volume and custom network should be checked, and the multi-container application
should be started with Docker Compose. The running application and container communication should
also be verified.

-> Docker Best Practice
The assignment recommends using minimal base images and a .dockerignore file to exclude
unnecessary files. This helps keep Docker images lightweight and can improve build and deployment
speed.

-> Conclusion
Week 4 provided practical experience with Docker containerization and Docker Compose. The activities
helped build an understanding of images, containers, volumes, networks and multi-container
applications. These fundamentals provide a useful foundation for future work involving Kubernetes,
CI/CD pipelines and cloud-native deployments.
